.SH NAME
whereis \- locate source, binary, and or manual for program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whereis
[
.B \-bms
] [
.B \-u
] [
.B \-BMS
dir ...
.B \-f
] 
.I file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Whereis
locates source/binary and manuals sections for specified files.
The supplied 
.I file
is first stripped of leading pathname components
and any (single) trailing extension of the form ".ext", e.g. ".c".
.B Whereis
then attempts to locate the desired program in a list of
standard places.
.sp
Finally, the
.B \-B
.B \-M
and
.B \-S
flags may be used to change or otherwise limit the places where
.B whereis
searches.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Search only for binaries.
.TP
.B \-f
Terminate the last such directory list
and signal the start of file names.
.TP
.B \-m
Search only for manual sections.
.TP
.B \-s
Search only for sources.
.TP
.B \-u
Used to search for unusual entries.
A file is said to be unusual if it does not have one entry of
each requested type.
Thus "whereis -m -u *" asks for those files in the current
directory which have no documentation.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following finds all the files in /usr/bin which are not documented
in /usr/man/man1 with source in /usr/src/cmd:
.IP
cd /usr/bin
.br
whereis \-u \-M /usr/man/man1 \-S /usr/src/cmd \-f *
.SH FILES
/usr/src/*
.br
/usr/{doc,man}/*
.br
/lib, /etc, /usr/{lib,bin}
.br
/z/bin
.SH LIMITATIONS
Since the program uses
.BR chdir (2)
to run faster, pathnames given with the
.B \-B
.B \-M
and
.B \-S
must be full; i.e. they must begin with a "/".
